  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Vertically stacked low pressure will meander off\nthe NC and Mid-Atlantic coast through mid week bringing\nprolonged strong north to northeasterly winds across ENC. \nGuidance continues to show the low wobbling a bit off the coast\nwhile deepening tonight as it slowly lifts northward off the \nMid- Atlantic coast, as the system remains cut-off from the mean\nflow aloft. Winds are expected to increase again this afternoon\nand evening, with another surge potentially on Wednesday, so \nwill make very little change to winds at this time. It is not \nuntil Thursday that the low will begin to accelerate away from \nthe East Coast as a northern stream shortwave digs into the Mid-\nAtlantic and Northeast. \n\nTight pressure gradients between the offshore low and high\npressure across the Eastern CONUS will bring strong north to\nnortheast winds across the region with the strongest winds\nacross the Outer Banks where gusts 45-50 mph will be possible. \nA Wind Advisory remains in effect from Duck to Ocracoke through \nthis evening. \n\nThe strong winds continue to bring large surf along the coast \nas well as coastal flooding impacts along vulnerable locations \nof the coast, especially along Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands \nwhere dune structure is fragile. The National Parks Service and\nNCDOT have recently reported substantial beach erosion from\nsouth Nags Head to Buxton, along with significant overwash\noccurring on side streets in Rodanthe. Due to hazardous road\nconditions and flooding, NC-12 remains closed from the Marc \nBasnight Bridge to Rodanthe, and ferry service remains \nsuspended in certain locations.\n\nEach subsequent high tide cycle will continue to threaten the \ndunes, further, and the threat of additional impacts to Hwy 12 \nremain. Minor soundside flooding is also a concern along \nsouthern portions of the Pamlico Sound, including Frisco, \nOcracoke, areas of Downeast Carteret Co. A Coastal Flood \nWarning remains in effect for Hatteras and Ocracoke Islands and \na Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect for the Northern \nOuter Banks and East Carteret County. Additionally, a high surf\nadvisory continues for Cape Lookout and points north through \nthe OBX. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Pleasantly cooler air mass that has advected in\nwill remain with us through the next several days with temps a \nbit below climo. Maximum temperatures through the period are \nexpected to remain in the 60-70 degree range. Overnight lows \nwill range from the 40s inland to the 50s coast.",
